<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Koliada</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Koleda, Kolęda, Kolenda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Koliada, or koleda, is the traditional Slavic name for the period from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epiphany-holiday">Epiphany</a> or, more generally, for Christmas-related rituals, some of which date to pre-Christian times. It denotes a festival held at the end of December to honour the sun at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice">winter solstice</a>, and one theory derives it from the ancient calendae. The word originated in Old Church Slavonic, and in several Slavic languages its meaning has shifted to the custom of going from house to house on Christmas Eve, when groups of singers called koledari sing carols and receive gifts in return. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a> kolenda, first mentioned in the 13th century, is one of the oldest recorded traditions of this kind in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koliada.jpg" alt="Koliada" width="330" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Konstantin Trutovsky · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trutovsky_Kolyadki.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rites followed the farming year, from <b>Koliada</b> at Christmastide to the fire and water festivals of Yarilo and Kupala.</blockquote><a href="/religion/slavic-paganism">Slavic paganism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Among the Slavs <b>Koliada</b> names the period from Christmas to Epiphany and rituals some of which date to pre-Christian times, and the profile of Slavic paganism names Koliada at Christmastide first among the rites that followed the farming year.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>Among the Slavs the name is <b>Koliada</b>, the traditional word for the period from Christmas to Epiphany or, more generally, for Christmas-related rituals, some of which date to pre-Christian times.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li></ul></section>
